WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.070
(pensive pop music)

00:00:04.412 --> 00:00:06.488
- [Narrator] About a
third of the United States

00:00:06.488 --> 00:00:08.875
is covered in forests.

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:13.026
In places like Colorado,
those forests are in danger.

00:00:13.026 --> 00:00:13.935
(flames crackling)

00:00:13.935 --> 00:00:14.912
There's fire.

00:00:14.912 --> 00:00:16.174
- [Woman] Our wildfires are getting

00:00:16.174 --> 00:00:18.371
more frequent and larger.

00:00:18.371 --> 00:00:20.366
- [Narrator] A devastating
beetle infestation.

00:00:20.366 --> 00:00:21.508
- Those trees are all dead.

00:00:21.508 --> 00:00:22.857
They don't have any branches anymore.

00:00:22.857 --> 00:00:25.111
They're just standing sticks.

00:00:25.111 --> 00:00:27.031
- [Narrator] And global warming.

00:00:27.031 --> 00:00:31.754
There is hope, and it's
found in an unlikely place.

00:00:31.754 --> 00:00:35.577
- When I tell people that I
work at a forest in Nebraska,

00:00:35.577 --> 00:00:37.695
they usually laugh and they're like,

00:00:37.695 --> 00:00:39.200
"Nebraska has a forest?"

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.700
(upbeat orchestral music)

00:00:45.034 --> 00:00:48.496
- [Narrator] Richard
Gilbert is a man on a quest.

00:00:48.496 --> 00:00:51.012
He's a biological
scientist who works here,

00:00:51.012 --> 00:00:55.367
at the Charles Bessey
Nursery in Halsey, Nebraska.

00:00:55.367 --> 00:00:59.809
It's the oldest federal
tree nursery in the U.S.

00:00:59.809 --> 00:01:02.269
The nursery's main mission
is to help preserve

00:01:02.269 --> 00:01:04.105
and repopulate national forests

00:01:04.105 --> 00:01:06.872
in the Rocky Mountain region.

00:01:06.872 --> 00:01:08.689
- Without Bessey,
they're not gonna be able

00:01:08.689 --> 00:01:10.679
to have the supply of trees

00:01:10.679 --> 00:01:13.458
that they need for reforestation.

00:01:13.458 --> 00:01:17.625
If they have a catastrophe,
we can be there to help them.

00:01:21.762 --> 00:01:26.409
What I love most about my job
is really the change in season

00:01:26.409 --> 00:01:28.762
and the change in work that I do.

00:01:28.762 --> 00:01:30.782
I get to work in the seed bank,

00:01:30.782 --> 00:01:33.119
I get to work in the
container part of this,

00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:34.844
and I get to work in the field,

00:01:34.844 --> 00:01:37.365
and I get to actually get my hands dirty.

00:01:37.365 --> 00:01:41.703
I don't just sit in an
office and push paper.

00:01:41.703 --> 00:01:42.880
- Richard's amazing.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.047
He really cares and he's always
trying to make it better.

00:01:50.224 --> 00:01:51.698
- We turn the greenhouses on,

00:01:51.698 --> 00:01:54.150
you got little seedlings germinating.

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.828
To me, it's spring.

00:01:55.828 --> 00:01:59.013
So, my spring starts really early

00:01:59.013 --> 00:02:01.430
and it's extremely rewarding.

00:02:02.391 --> 00:02:04.031
- He's very proud of his work.

00:02:04.031 --> 00:02:06.352
He's very proud of his trees.

00:02:06.352 --> 00:02:09.092
- I love growing plants
and helping them grow.

00:02:09.092 --> 00:02:13.259
I don't ever wanna lose that
contact with the plants.

00:02:18.886 --> 00:02:20.685
When they need 'em, we grow 'em,

00:02:20.685 --> 00:02:25.094
and we get 'em produced
in less than a year.

00:02:25.094 --> 00:02:27.858
All of the trees are
produced in the greenhouses

00:02:27.858 --> 00:02:30.339
and then they are brought
in here on tables.

00:02:30.339 --> 00:02:33.698
This Y is okay 'cause it's above halfway

00:02:33.698 --> 00:02:36.077
and we have a central leader.

00:02:36.077 --> 00:02:40.244
We can pack 110,000 to 120,000
trees per belt per day.

00:02:43.064 --> 00:02:44.960
We're packing container trees

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.921
for the San Juan National Forest.

00:02:47.921 --> 00:02:50.557
They don't get their moisture
in the spring a lotta times.

00:02:50.557 --> 00:02:52.699
They get their summer monsoon seasons.

00:02:52.699 --> 00:02:55.324
That's when they want
their seedlings delivered.

00:02:55.324 --> 00:02:58.239
- Richard remembers
all of this information

00:02:58.239 --> 00:03:00.849
and wants the feedback and
wants to know how they did.

00:03:00.849 --> 00:03:05.467
At our best, we might
get 70%, 80% survival.

00:03:05.467 --> 00:03:07.710
- Without Bessey, they're
not gonna be able to have

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:11.877
the supply of trees that
they need for reforestation.

00:03:23.342 --> 00:03:25.129
Plants are just amazing

00:03:25.129 --> 00:03:27.056
and there are so many of 'em out there,

00:03:27.056 --> 00:03:29.581
and I will never know how
to grow every single one.

00:03:29.581 --> 00:03:32.065
If it's a very, very interesting plant,

00:03:32.065 --> 00:03:34.458
I'll try to produce it
and grow it at the house.

00:03:34.458 --> 00:03:36.500
And if you guys drive
by my house over there,

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:37.852
you'll understand;

00:03:37.852 --> 00:03:39.130
you'll see all of the different plants

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:41.047
that I have over there.

